<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Linderhof Palace</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1869">1869</a>–<a href="/years/1878">1878</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Linderhof Palace is a small rococo-style palace near Ettal in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavarian-alps">Bavarian Alps</a>, built for King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ludwig-ii-of-bavaria">Ludwig II of Bavaria</a> between 1869 and 1878. It was the only one of his palaces completed in his lifetime and the one in which he lived longest. Its park holds fantasy buildings such as the Venus Grotto, an artificial cave inspired by Wagner's Tannhäuser, and a Moorish kiosk.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/linderhof-palace.jpg" alt="Linderhof Palace" width="320" height="199" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Softeis · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Linderhof-1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Linderhof lies about 10 km west of the village of Ettal in south-west <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> and is the smallest of the three palaces of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ludwig-ii-of-bavaria">Ludwig II of Bavaria</a>. Ludwig knew the area from his youth, when he accompanied his father Maximilian II on hunting trips. On becoming king in 1864 he inherited a hunting lodge, the Königshäuschen, and in 1869 began enlarging it. In 1874 he decided to demolish it and rebuild it at its present place in the park, adding three new rooms and the staircase and cladding the wooden exterior in stone. The building follows the style of the second rococo. A total of 8,460,937 marks was spent on Linderhof between 1863 and 1886.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Linderhof was inspired by the palace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a>, whom Ludwig took as an idol, and its style follows the mid-18th-century rococo of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xv">Louis XV</a>, being based more directly on the Petit Trianon. The sun is a motif of the decoration and stands for the French idea of absolutism, which Ludwig saw as the ideal of a God-given monarchy but which the constitution of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> did not allow. The palace has a private atmosphere, with only four rooms of real function. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hall-of-mirrors">Hall of Mirrors</a> was a drawing room where Ludwig read through the night.</p><p>The dining room has a table on a lifting platform, called Tischlein deck dich, so that he could dine without servants, although the table was laid for four, since he is said to have talked to imaginary guests such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xv">Louis XV</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marie-antoinette">Marie Antoinette</a>. The bedroom, the largest chamber, faces north, which reflects his image of himself as a Night-King, since he turned night into day. It was completely rebuilt in 1884 and could not be finished before his death two years later.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The park, laid out by the Court Garden Director Carl von Effner, combines formal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> gardens with landscaped sections like an English garden. The Venus Grotto, built between 1875 and 1877 under the opera set designer August Dirigl, is an iron construction covered in canvas and cement, made as an illustration of the first act of Wagner's Tannhäuser. It had a lake, a waterfall and a wave machine, and in 1878 dynamos driven by a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steam-engine">steam engine</a> were installed by Johann Sigmund Schuckert to light it. Hunding's Hut was inspired by Wagner's Die Walküre. In 2025 the palace was designated a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Linderhof Palace” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ludwig II made Munich the scene of Richard Wagner's Tristan und Isolde in 1865 and helped finance the festival theatre at Bayreuth, and his castles of Neuschwanstein, <b>Linderhof</b> and Herrenchiemsee became some of the most visited sights in Europe.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-bavaria">Kingdom of Bavaria · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
